{
  "summary": "Critiqued the two-tier synthesis, identifying several underexplored weaknesses: (1) accumulated feedback creates a conflicting signals problem when prior reviews give contradictory guidance, which can degrade agent performance rather than improve it; (2) the 'penultimate attempt' diagnostic prompt injects overhead into an attempt that might succeed anyway and relies on knowing max attempts at prompt-construction time; (3) the claim that the working agent is best-positioned to diagnose its own failures is questionable since the reason retries fail is often a persistent blind spot that self-analysis won't fix; (4) the plan review loop and impl/test/review loop have fundamentally different retry semantics that a one-size-fits-all accumulated feedback approach may not serve equally well; (5) the '~30-line change' estimate is understated since _get_latest_feedback is called in at least four distinct call sites each feeding different prompt templates; and (6) none of the three prior contributions address the meta-question of when a feature should be recognized as fundamentally intractable for automated resolution rather than just exhausting retries. The most actionable improvement would be a focused version of Tier 1 that provides a structured failure summary (attempt count, whether the same issue recurred, whether feedback was contradictory) rather than dumping all raw feedback, combined with a simple stuck-detection heuristic that flags features where the core complaint hasn't changed across 3+ attempts."
